#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f0520e86-8df9-5090-8d5c-761a4ba20583" :question/math-academy-id "e-6565" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "45688683-2788-58bd-8cf5-80feab863551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6566" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1826d315-cef0-5b5f-b654-61733a63bf2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6567" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "4f4f4539-466a-5cc0-9470-8b94d6221093" :question/math-academy-id "e-6568" :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7adc37d-388e-504b-8308-68d35a0c3f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388427} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9a7ccb-a6b9-56e9-a827-933c59d3317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388428} {:answer/id #uuid "dd956161-9bbf-50bc-b1ca-a04c91195ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388429} {:answer/id #uuid "a68c29a5-d903-5575-ac88-48882c10bbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388430} {:answer/id #uuid "9f907570-32c5-5a61-a451-65ce524675e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b9a7ccb-a6b9-56e9-a827-933c59d3317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388428} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b498458-1211-55f1-a377-d1feeb14a65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e02865a-5c8d-59a5-b60c-4dc61a1f54cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-130670" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{″}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}-y=1$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}-y=1\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=2,b(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,c(x)=-1,$ and $f(x)=1.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)={x}^{2},$ and $f(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d426fd3-3a43-565b-a3a6-9730a1480e0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0c1bc8-571d-588d-b7a1-a8049bb9dbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388435} {:answer/id #uuid "122a8b52-363d-5714-b1d3-022de781fd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388436} {:answer/id #uuid "947ef32d-7645-5d35-8b54-f4ad6c87f060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388437} {:answer/id #uuid "3152606a-f1ec-5468-bc88-d7eea6b86e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d426fd3-3a43-565b-a3a6-9730a1480e0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} :answer-field/id #uuid "959ce921-7783-5c1a-980d-e31ebfae0671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39f9da72-d680-558d-9f90-b3e43effce25" :question/math-academy-id "q-130677" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0$\n- $2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{y}^{′}+{y}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+{x}^{3}{y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=2,b(x)={x}^{3},c(x)=2x,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}=x{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=x,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=x{e}^{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0e4a815-12a7-5e90-8b07-ce78cbefe34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{2x}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388441} {:answer/id #uuid "e3dc1170-59a6-5448-9d15-23b38be7d712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+xy=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388442} {:answer/id #uuid "dec0aff9-77d5-55ac-8ef7-57de5543f9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}-\\frac{3}{x}y+y=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388443} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad5ce83-f2f0-5bba-be6e-7e56300e9c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}{y}^{″}-3{e}^{-2x}+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388444} {:answer/id #uuid "0faf2c69-c122-53c2-95b6-d0338851df8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3dc1170-59a6-5448-9d15-23b38be7d712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+xy=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388442} :answer-field/id #uuid "192de820-9c9a-502b-b0d7-7b5d93dc7105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b1844a4-8dd8-5c43-976b-3a111b7e543e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130990" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+xy=2{e}^{2x},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+xy=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb07061d-f07f-57a7-b984-fc5ffa94b82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388448} {:answer/id #uuid "75df4b17-8fa6-5d19-9812-cee28dfc3fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+\\frac{8}{x}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388449} {:answer/id #uuid "548bd94c-48ae-5ae4-be8d-7e95925d3eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388450} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3f8868-ba58-51d4-a693-416e3f199152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-\\frac{5}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{8}{x}y=\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388451} {:answer/id #uuid "434985a7-97b1-5e4a-8571-30c168cb53d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+8y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548bd94c-48ae-5ae4-be8d-7e95925d3eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388450} :answer-field/id #uuid "7728e383-2210-54a0-8958-18e81efc881e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79885437-9cb7-526b-b102-1b3c0afd3706" :question/math-academy-id "q-130991" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=2x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} {:answer/id #uuid "3558e504-c815-5bdb-904b-6b499c98a79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} :answer-field/id #uuid "600fbb74-bdbd-5116-97b9-b2a5cd79d9b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} {:answer/id #uuid "de118a19-6e65-5980-a6d7-0def901542e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e245b17-4874-510f-a799-adb5a6f3f08b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} {:answer/id #uuid "5edabb40-bdd5-5070-97d3-bcda1993b06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c68ad2-f693-5f7e-ba69-d5b504b3c6d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e93ecbe-42b9-5aed-a2ce-1b2b418237f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-337990" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=-x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0755616e-8d6f-50ce-82cd-8c3124d45f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341388} {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389} :answer-field/id #uuid "64dfac85-867c-5d93-abdd-a093526fff34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} {:answer/id #uuid "2e727099-ce63-54df-b3c8-6be4852bed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a7bfec-c698-5162-9d1d-70b6585ae610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e407a07-7097-5845-9355-c9ad8823438e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341394} {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee27974-b815-5e20-b40c-bd897c398114" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fc00c12-63c8-559a-aa45-1b909350bd43" :question/math-academy-id "q-337996" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=7,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-{x}^{3},$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0fd996-c57e-5139-a4f4-90ca5c65f5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375484} {:answer/id #uuid "a104965a-e982-5d79-81df-5a485c54f2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0fd996-c57e-5139-a4f4-90ca5c65f5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375484} :answer-field/id #uuid "c117b3c3-c2d3-5439-811c-aa143241f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375483} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68e19d30-2771-5e78-92d1-42f0e84dc711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375487} {:answer/id #uuid "78e1f85e-ad80-5a7c-9fa5-a0cd67b8aab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e1f85e-ad80-5a7c-9fa5-a0cd67b8aab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8829219e-1bf2-5f9f-930a-f33f21ba659f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d89d41e-d0de-5fa0-a519-0bbafbf9e73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375490} {:answer/id #uuid "8eecc096-b03c-5a44-8e32-37a248a69cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eecc096-b03c-5a44-8e32-37a248a69cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3566069-abad-563e-a36c-639688c12233" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da06f22-6a38-578e-b590-3e2d4a9025f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-338004" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy=-4x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-4x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ad6cc1-a74b-5a4a-a6b7-f2475d4d8a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388455} {:answer/id #uuid "68d95188-9d4f-5d7d-8106-188b09761a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d95188-9d4f-5d7d-8106-188b09761a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388456} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33760c5-1d2c-5b3d-a842-a65848ab0494" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51745c22-4166-587a-8f8c-ff5f872f4679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388458} {:answer/id #uuid "ed33b2af-85a8-5043-b4c7-15a3c937933b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51745c22-4166-587a-8f8c-ff5f872f4679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388458} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42a3c75-ef36-574f-916b-9d53e3ff7918" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7721cf4d-98f8-5f5e-b4de-6a88d5f85dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388461} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba9708c-0749-5f1f-98b4-16821f3f9d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ba9708c-0749-5f1f-98b4-16821f3f9d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388462} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16a0680-04e8-502a-92fe-24bfd04ab5c0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0dc4a39a-133c-5d88-913a-6b2fafc59819" :question/math-academy-id "q-338023" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-{e}^{x}=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy-{e}^{x}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c052085f-57d9-5b57-a536-9b5045df25de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388465} {:answer/id #uuid "08e081dd-2352-56a7-af9a-2486cdb77e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e081dd-2352-56a7-af9a-2486cdb77e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388466} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb792e9f-5a42-5809-8b49-600b3cb18c2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda99050-4f18-54d3-82a9-5abe1e5067b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388468} {:answer/id #uuid "ed367e1c-43a9-5c5b-8b48-814242043534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda99050-4f18-54d3-82a9-5abe1e5067b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388468} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b314b8-fdf8-5685-8586-31146cc111d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388467} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff6c13fc-1344-5ec0-9092-92309e4119da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388471} {:answer/id #uuid "0c636cd5-a9f1-50b6-85f6-13845b2bf719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c636cd5-a9f1-50b6-85f6-13845b2bf719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388472} :answer-field/id #uuid "3444c338-3333-58af-99c3-618c62175834" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8b12875-ae8a-5464-b1d4-8be3ec87c5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338082" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2y=x$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y=-5x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-5x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47bad2b8-8d3e-57d6-82b3-2c3439f672a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341418} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff67c9-ece9-59f1-8612-b8e2b3c84de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341419} {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} {:answer/id #uuid "7558911d-9966-59ef-82bc-a27c1567a02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341421} {:answer/id #uuid "7aca29ef-7d33-5dd9-8aa3-21f2f8e57080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} :answer-field/id #uuid "6626197e-a518-52e5-8075-49de48d89a58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63a0e75a-e8ed-5555-a126-d694412fb15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338102" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c24394-e973-59d1-9167-634cf777f21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341432} {:answer/id #uuid "8802af8b-4dde-5a58-a51e-084a51ffb842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341433} {:answer/id #uuid "2abac178-ade9-5f83-9fe1-6252892ec145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341434} {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d74180-5d78-5e19-a40e-3203ce946229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a9729b-f977-5b73-a7a7-48f5c43c8f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec6d918c-1e89-56ba-9337-5b4cab88266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21cd639a-6016-5148-85cd-597f84de37a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388475} {:answer/id #uuid "757e6245-31da-5dee-849d-87197bbd53e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388476} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf3f780-7239-597b-8314-7cff393c9227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388477} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a73aea-b259-5b50-b001-b97fdf398653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388478} {:answer/id #uuid "81568e55-c63e-53fb-98ec-49385e1a62d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757e6245-31da-5dee-849d-87197bbd53e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388476} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0eaa757-e0cf-59cf-80d3-0d795152d5f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c104c7c7-be1b-5f7c-9495-548d3d354240" :question/math-academy-id "q-339251" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,y(4)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=-1$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=9$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-8x{y}^{′}+16y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f82afe-9d1e-508a-9934-7acc10eee8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388482} {:answer/id #uuid "c2652419-e6d8-5bd2-b5dc-d99e3c25f8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388483} {:answer/id #uuid "0119afe8-c46e-5e5f-86eb-d69383a39598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388484} {:answer/id #uuid "d7abb2b7-c5d2-5947-8436-04db433d5af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388485} {:answer/id #uuid "1262a805-a917-5f42-a9fb-20fce50de6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1262a805-a917-5f42-a9fb-20fce50de6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388486} :answer-field/id #uuid "477b451b-0522-5f96-ac66-21a2fc3bb2af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a96f0dc8-d72a-5771-ac70-fe78350fbed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339253" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=4x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+9y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=-2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3752d86c-7a3b-5c0e-9a51-0507c31bc467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375494} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb8a2a-230c-5cf3-bff7-a3994bb049be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375495} {:answer/id #uuid "ba04aeca-04dc-5e8f-9ba3-261b61a26a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375496} {:answer/id #uuid "42266fdf-5a26-58dc-89c4-602074a77807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375497} {:answer/id #uuid "24d63809-e024-53f3-87cc-61435b10644a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba04aeca-04dc-5e8f-9ba3-261b61a26a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375496} :answer-field/id #uuid "11078401-779e-5895-a7c6-38aec08aefe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8f4d7ab-4946-517f-88cd-116b43135b3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339254" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y=8$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem.  It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188e19b8-04d9-5024-a1a0-560681b52a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341439} {:answer/id #uuid "3557fe35-3d8a-5b9f-8319-034a0b55e292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341440} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} {:answer/id #uuid "5c65fb1c-74b3-5c07-a63e-9a134cc05155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341442} {:answer/id #uuid "7c29f87b-9169-55a0-9fcd-db3d482e3a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cf2381-7b48-5325-b87b-78fc4e994f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b3292e-ae09-5cdf-bbb6-9d5f24a711b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339256" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+2y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=5x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=4$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b3c576-b762-562b-99cd-9c3ba02ccc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388489} {:answer/id #uuid "22b9db78-0fd0-51bd-8bb0-30c2af5903f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388490} {:answer/id #uuid "076c6d5a-b8a1-5254-a119-19556c58d65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388491} {:answer/id #uuid "bab6a1e4-3888-5e17-8278-d3043ed15a84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388492} {:answer/id #uuid "3e84a847-6c69-52f6-a914-81ec36694650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "076c6d5a-b8a1-5254-a119-19556c58d65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388491} :answer-field/id #uuid "5297bffe-01e4-5540-805b-2cfd507c3611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "475611da-016f-5cb2-8388-61fb23e081e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65009" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${e}^{x}{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $3{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+4{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+4{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $4{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be80b6d-fc43-5fee-819f-e1e38a7a54bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388496} {:answer/id #uuid "647b8314-ff72-5602-9143-2100dd5e2a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{′}+8y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388497} {:answer/id #uuid "698725f6-3e1e-5ccd-a850-1d6e8f780f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388498} {:answer/id #uuid "e0221153-3472-5cfa-9c82-7a57639ded48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+16y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388499} {:answer/id #uuid "345a1fa4-485e-5214-a0da-ebc030339800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{′}+8y=\\frac{x+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0221153-3472-5cfa-9c82-7a57639ded48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+16y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388499} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf53b5b-2d9c-5fe4-8ca6-b0733998622f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abb0887e-440c-5ffe-8cd4-bd9f5b81ff20" :question/math-academy-id "q-65016" :question/problem "For the differential equation $2{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+16y=x+1,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+16y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d0066a-3c21-5721-9abc-00fd17ab504b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}{y}^{″}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}{y}^{′}+y=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388503} {:answer/id #uuid "8b41706d-a63d-5d7a-93a8-e0b928c3017a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{x}^{3}y}{8}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388504} {:answer/id #uuid "6cebe2cc-ef0a-5a01-9def-49787afd66ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388505} {:answer/id #uuid "42886a47-1c3e-5dd4-8a2d-33acda3101ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{x}^{3}y}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388506} {:answer/id #uuid "da70b27e-f373-5c19-82a4-5cfe54fb50d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cebe2cc-ef0a-5a01-9def-49787afd66ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388505} :answer-field/id #uuid "755468de-194f-5f15-a1af-6399c4248d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c688f5cb-d2b1-599c-a9dd-2876785b3bd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-65019" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=8,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} {:answer/id #uuid "796af2d8-3cb3-5bf0-9137-370ed0d9102b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341454} {:answer/id #uuid "945961fe-2c6d-529c-87e0-7ab2efe873bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341455} {:answer/id #uuid "7d866803-b1f6-5fe1-bbea-38c8c0b7bbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341456} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b145a-16e6-5f2a-aaf7-4f776e4acff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4ced10-83a3-51c8-9db0-13b6654e303b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01fc92bc-36e8-54db-9a2e-368326538d04" :question/math-academy-id "q-93721" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62ffba74-a557-5c89-b488-0e28b12db034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388510} {:answer/id #uuid "7493c4dc-92f1-5093-9637-e8e3cd48b560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388511} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9cdfd5-57f6-55a9-8af4-d7e0906ab00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388512} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f0183d-735a-5bc1-961c-e83597ccb177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388513} {:answer/id #uuid "bdec2677-4461-5946-b150-262643d4d4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6f0183d-735a-5bc1-961c-e83597ccb177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388513} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1bdbcf-322c-5d98-a32c-96d5b7eb17a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c974cfe0-a93c-50e7-822f-ecbadcdf917a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93779" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=16,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y=0.\n$$"}]}
